  "explanation": "byteorder 1.5.0: fixed-width unsigned and signed integer writes, plus BE/LE write_f32/write_f64, write their exact bit representations in the selected endian order and preserve trailing bytes when the output is long enough; shorter buffers panic before writing. Float proofs quantify over every 32/64-bit representation, including NaN payloads and signed zeros, under explicit bit-pattern and std to_bits models. No floating arithmetic is modeled. BE/LE read_u24 additionally returns the exact unsigned24 prefix decode below2^24, and asserts on shorter input. LittleEndian from_slice_u16/u32/u64/u128 additionally leave every slice unchanged on the verified little-endian target, including empty slices and arbitrary unsigned values. The two handwritten Default methods always panic. Four unsigned variable-width readers decode the exact selected prefix under their complete byte-count/input-length guard, and assert otherwise. ",
  "limitations": "This covers 20 selected writer methods, six unsigned readers, four native-target unsigned identity conversions, and two handwritten Default methods, not all public APIs. Fixed 16/32/64/128-bit reads, signed readers, variable-width writers, other bulk and in-place conversions, I/O extensions, other targets and release extraction remain unproved. The float result establishes bit serialization only: floating arithmetic, comparisons, rounding, numeric accuracy, NaN arithmetic and signaling behavior are not modeled. Kani public-writer buffer lengths are fixed at 6 and 10; arbitrary valid slice lengths are quantified separately by the Lean theorems. The signed from_slice defaults contain unsafe slice reinterpretation and are not covered; the floating conversions and opposite-endian/other-target paths are also unproved. The byteorder-lite eight-method result is not assumed transferable across versions. Panic messages and unwinding side effects are not specified.",
